# NOTICE

mkit builds on ideas and source material from two MIT-licensed projects. Both
original projects are worth reading.

## repository-harness — hoangnb24

https://github.com/hoangnb24/repository-harness
MIT License, Copyright (c) hoangnb24

mkit inherits three mechanisms from this project:

- the authority gate, which stops before edits when policy has no owner-approved
  answer, including the rule that configurable defaults are not authority;
- work-shape classification, which keeps process proportional to the task; and
- completion based on observable evidence rather than descriptions.

The `decision.md` structure and `Proposed / Accepted / Superseded / Rejected`
lifecycle come directly from `docs/templates/decision.md`. The marked-block
mechanism in `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` comes from `HARNESS:BEGIN` and
`HARNESS:END`.

mkit does not use the original project's Rust CLI or SQLite layer. Decision 0022
froze that layer, and decision 0019 moved the default toward Git-native documents.

## skills — Matt Pocock

https://github.com/mattpocock/skills
MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Matt Pocock

The `mkit:grill-me` skill is a fork of `grilling`. It preserves the design tree,
the frontier concept, the question format with a recommended answer, and the
rule that finding facts belongs to the agent while decisions belong to the user.

It differs from the original in two ways because its intended user is different:

- it asks one question at a time instead of the whole frontier in one round; and
- every option must state concrete consequences without technical jargon.
